# Pelthos Therapeutics to Combine with Castle Creek Biosciences, Creating Transformative Biotechnology Company Focused on Serious Diseases that Affect the Skin


October 30, 2026 9:09am EDT[Download as PDF](https://d1io3yog0oux5.cloudfront.net/_f1347042c5d0430067c1d418ff0da091/pelthos/news/2026-10-30_Pelthos_Therapeutics_to_Combine_with_Castle_Creek__65.pdf)


*Strategic Combination Brings Highly Innovative Late-Stage D-Fi Franchise Targeting Rare Diseases, with Orphan Drug-Designated Lead Indication in Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (DEB)*


*Combines Two Complementary Platforms Across Therapeutic Modalities: Castle Creek’s Fibroblast-Based Cell and Gene Therapy Technology with Pelthos’ Novel Platform*


DURHAM, N.C. and EXTON, Pa., Oct. 30, 2026 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- Pelthos Therapeutics Inc. (NYSE American: PTHS) (“Pelthos” or the “Company”), a biopharmaceutical company committed to commercializing innovative therapeutic products for unmet patient needs, and Castle Creek Biosciences, Inc. (“Castle Creek”), a late-stage company developing redosable gene therapies, today announced they have entered into a definitive agreement under which Castle Creek will merge with Pelthos. The combination creates a transformative biotechnology company with novel technologies, including cell and gene therapy, targeting serious diseases that affect the skin. Castle Creek’s late-stage fibroblast-based platform complements Pelthos’ novel FDA-approved product portfolio and established commercial capabilities in dermatology. Upon close, the combined company will operate as Pelthos Therapeutics Inc. and continue to trade on the NYSE American (ticker symbol: “PTHS”).


Castle Creek’s lead candidate is D-Fi (dabocemagene autoficel; FCX-007), an injectable, redosable, gene-modified cell therapy with a continued Phase 3 study planned for the treatment of dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (DEB), a devastating rare inherited disease caused by mutations in the COL7A1 gene. There is significant demand for new treatment options in DEB given the substantial burden of untreated wounds and increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma. D-Fi, which has received Orphan Drug Designation, is administered by intradermal injection every other month, and the drug product delivers type VII collagen (COL7) producing cells. In addition, Castle Creek is using its proprietary fibroblast technology to develop a topical formulation of D-Fi and to pursue additional therapeutic targets in serious diseases that affect the skin, including malignancies and inflammatory conditions.


“This transformative combination brings a novel platform with significant growth potential to Pelthos and broadens our focus beyond cutaneous infectious diseases to serious diseases that affect the skin,” said Scott Plesha, CEO of Pelthos. “D-Fi builds on our established strength in dermatology, especially within pediatric dermatology, which we believe will accelerate its launch and value in the marketplace. This transaction creates a stronger, more diversified biotechnology company positioned to bring important new therapies to patients and drive long-term value for our shareholders.”


“D-Fi has the potential to become a blockbuster franchise in DEB, where patients still have significant unmet needs,” said Matt Gantz, Chairman and CEO of Castle Creek. “Joining with Pelthos will provide the commercial infrastructure to help realize the D-Fi opportunity and accelerate the pipeline of new indications from our platform.”


**Strategic Rationale**


- **Compelling Complementary Capabilities:** As part of Pelthos’ portfolio, D-Fi will leverage the Company’s established commercial leadership, deep expertise in dermatology, and existing physician relationships to accelerate its commercial launch and delivery of important therapies to patients.


- **Greater Pipeline Diversification:** The combination brings together Pelthos’ three novel FDA-approved products, ZELSUVMI®, XEPI® and XEGLYZE®, with D-Fi, creating a portfolio spanning marketed, launch-stage and late-stage assets across multiple therapeutic modalities. This broader mix provides multiple potential sources of growth over different time horizons.


- **Expanded Technology Base:** Castle Creek’s fibroblast technology adds a cell and gene therapy capability to Pelthos’ existing Nitricil platform, giving the combined company multiple therapeutic modalities to address serious diseases that affect the skin where conventional small molecules have had limited impact and expand the pipeline over time.


**Transaction Terms**


Under the terms of the agreement, Castle Creek stockholders will receive 5,200,000 shares of Pelthos common stock at the closing of the transaction, representing approximately 32.5% of Pelthos’ fully diluted shares. As defined within the agreement, and subject to meeting various developmental and sales-based criteria, Castle Creek stockholders will also be eligible to receive: (i) up to 5,800,000 shares of Pelthos common stock upon FDA approval of D-Fi; (ii) up to a total of $300 million in milestone payments, payable in cash (or, to the extent required to preserve the intended tax treatment of the transaction, in a combination of cash and shares of Pelthos common stock), including a $50 million payment due upon achievement of specified Phase 3 clinical endpoints, and up to $250 million in additional consideration tied to D-Fi’s first commercial sale and achievement of specified net sales milestones; and (iii) 80% of the net proceeds from the sale of any priority review voucher awarded upon FDA approval of D-Fi.


The transaction has been approved by the boards of directors of both companies and is expected to close by the end of the first quarter of 2027, subject to customary closing conditions including approval by Castle Creek’s stockholders. Scott Plesha will continue to serve as Chief Executive Officer, and Peter Greenleaf will serve as Executive Chair of the combined company. Upon closing, the combined company’s board will consist of 10 directors, including two designated by Castle Creek.


**Advisors**


Latham & Watkins LLP is serving as legal advisor to Pelthos. Oppenheimer & Co. Inc. provided a fairness opinion to Pelthos’ board of directors. Bradley Arant Boult Cummings LLP and Katten Muchin Rosenman LLP are serving as legal advisors to Castle Creek and PHCP, LLC is serving as financial advisor to Castle Creek. Collected Strategies is serving as strategic communications advisor to Pelthos.


**About D-Fi**
D-Fi (dabocemagene autoficel; FCX-007) is an injectable autologous gene-modified cell therapy candidate for the treatment of DEB, a devastating, progressive, painful and debilitating rare genetic skin disorder. DEB is caused by a mutation in the COL7A1 gene, leading to a deficiency of normal type VII collagen (COL7) protein, impairing the connection between the epidermis and the dermis. D-Fi is composed of a patient’s own dermal fibroblasts, which are genetically modified completely ex vivo with a self-inactivating (SIN) lentiviral vector (LV) containing the COL7A1 gene to express COL7. D-Fi is locally administered by intradermal injection into chronic wounds where the COL7 protein can support the formation of anchoring fibrils in the skin. In clinical studies, D-Fi has been generally well tolerated, with injection site reactions (skin discoloration, erythema, hemorrhage, pain, and swelling) being the most commonly reported adverse drug reactions. D-Fi has Orphan Drug Designation for the treatment of DEB and Rare Pediatric Disease, Fast Track, and Regenerative Medicine Advanced Therapy designations for the treatment of recessive DEB (RDEB) from the FDA.


**About Castle Creek Biosciences, Inc.**
Castle Creek Biosciences is a late-stage company developing re-dosable gene therapies. It is preparing to advance the Phase 3 program of its lead candidate, D-Fi (FCX-007), for patients with dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (DEB). Learn more at[castlecreekbio.com](https://www.globenewswire.com/Tracker?data=i_F2M0XWNH9BK6aiuIl3e49Si98c-CqEEqNPQT2eVrdNL5P-juPEA3nXqICk8wGWTx5tD3eM4IOi2_Ub_1pICCHV-c3IocvTSXhT4BlwgYE=) .


**About Pelthos Therapeutics**
Pelthos Therapeutics is a commercial-stage biopharmaceutical company focused on building and advancing a portfolio of differentiated cutaneous infectious disease products that address unmet patient needs. ZELSUVMI® (berdazimer) topical gel, 10.3%, the Company’s lead product, is the first and only prescription therapy approved for use at home by patients, parents, and caregivers to treat molluscum contagiosum. The Company’s portfolio of assets includes XEPI® (ozenoxacin) Cream, 1%, a topical treatment for impetigo, and XEGLYZE® (abametapir), a topical treatment for head lice. More information is available at[www.pelthos.com](https://www.globenewswire.com/Tracker?data=FpY37IPjuSaZNOKddb4bjTxuyU8npWrZk2qPX4U37yPPj_wh-Kr7F6qUIAQFpKWPlDKWbqzLyFsV83wjTnvdWnKcp9_tI2hC71z-00eJy1o=) . Follow Pelthos on[LinkedIn](https://www.globenewswire.com/Tracker?data=-nN7hJZ_jiu_heoWZFlvkLQaSsTprhLTfkifiSEraT1yM6-bvpa3GJ1hBayTOtls6krCDddjqHmHozGYEoDtQOcFxyzsfkLkbCMILvGS1aQ=) and[X](https://www.globenewswire.com/Tracker?data=h423A6ZtaLizcAxD9PTLDTEAGDCtqnn8H3m64VYGUEXS6W1UDchUOLqNShQp6YnfvCRTbEd9Yd5U7JOddkZNTg==) .


